<%/* The items grid — the most intricate markup in the repo, and exactly the
     code you do not want two divergent copies of. 6 to 9 columns, three of them
     conditional; a banner row that must span its own table exactly; a component
     indent derived from `path`; the `??`-not-`||` discounted-subtotal rule; and
     the em-dash-on-a-taxed-zero cell.

     Props — every one of them is a decision the DOCUMENT makes, handed in:
       renderedItems         the rows to draw, dividers interleaved
       columnCount           how wide a banner spans (see `bannerSpan` below)
       hasRentals/hasDiscount/hasTax   which conditional columns are on
       rolledUp              money moved off hidden zero-priced components
       componentDepth        nesting depth UNDER A LINE ITEM, divider-agnostic
       numberCell/moneyCell/moneyCellOn/itemTaxCents/pathKey   the cell rules
       DISAMBIGUATED_ITEM_TYPES        which `type` values the Type column says
       showDestinationBanners          suppressed at a single destination
       showOrderBanner                 suppressed at a single source order
       destinationDetail     OPT-IN (default off): draw each destination
                             divider as a full section — name, active
                             window, charge window, and both endpoints with
                             address, contact and instructions — the way the
                             manager OrderDetail items table does
                             (manager 73f625c). Off, the arm below is the
                             plain name banner, unchanged.
       destinations          the document destination pairs, joined to a
                             divider by `uid` (core `assignDestinationPairUids`)
       u                     the family util namespace (legend, same-dates)
       hasCollection         whether a collection leg exists at all

     ⚠️ **Hierarchy-agnostic on purpose.** `componentDepth` already filters
     divider uids out of the path, so this partial is indifferent to HOW MANY
     divider levels exist — `[destination, group]` on an order,
     `[order, destination, group]` on an invoice. api-cloudrun#538 would re-base
     orders onto the invoice's three levels; when it lands, nothing here moves.

     Both banner arms are gated on the same principle — a divider that names the
     only member of its level is noise — but each gate is the CALLER's to
     compute, because only the caller knows what its level contains. The quote
     has no order level at all and passes nothing.

     ⚠️ **Multi-order invoicing is not live**: it is a manager feature landing
     after the CRMS cutover, so every invoice today bills exactly one order and
     the order banner renders on none of them. The arm is here so that the
     feature does not need this shared file reopened. */%>
<%
const {
  renderedItems, columnCount, hasRentals, hasDiscount, hasTax,
  rolledUp, componentDepth, numberCell, moneyCell, moneyCellOn,
  itemTaxCents, pathKey, DISAMBIGUATED_ITEM_TYPES,
  showDestinationBanners, showOrderBanner,
  destinationDetail = false, destinations = [], u, hasCollection = false,
} = it;
%>
<%/* THE HEADER ROW HAS TWO HOMES, and which one is a fact about
     `destinationDetail` alone.

     OFF — every family but the quote — it is the table’s one <thead>, and the
     rendered markup is what it has always been, so no other family’s golden
     moves.

     ON, each destination section is followed by its OWN header row, so the
     section reads as the heading of the lines under it rather than as a row
     inside a table that was already headed above it. Two consequences, neither
     optional:

     - Those header rows live in <tbody>, NOT in a second <thead>. Only the first
       table-header-group is a header group — the rest render as row groups — and
       the first one REPEATS at the top of every printed page, so a <thead> here
       would reprint destination 1’s address block above destination 2’s lines.
     - `table-layout: fixed` sizes a table from its FIRST ROW, which is now the
       destination section’s single colspan cell — Blink would split that evenly
       across every column and the grid would lose `--label-col`. The <colgroup>
       states the widths instead, so sizing no longer depends on which KIND of row
       happens to come first. It is emitted only in this shape: the headed table
       is sized by its header row exactly as before.

     Built as a string because it is emitted from two places and a second copy of
     the conditional columns is a second thing to keep in step with `columnCount`. */%>
<%
const headerRow = '<tr class="column-headers">' + [
  '<th scope="col" class="wide">Item Description</th>',
  '<th scope="col">Type</th>',
  '<th scope="col">Qty</th>',
  hasRentals ? '<th scope="col">Duration</th>' : '',
  '<th scope="col">Unit Price</th>',
  hasDiscount ? '<th scope="col">Discount</th>' : '',
  '<th scope="col">Subtotal</th>',
  hasTax ? '<th scope="col">Tax</th>' : '',
  '<th scope="col" class="total">Total</th>',
].filter(Boolean).join("\n        ") + '</tr>';

// TRUE until something has been headed. A destination section emits the header
// under itself; anything reaching a line, a group banner or a destination that
// resolves to no section pays for it first, so a grid whose rows do not open
// with a destination is still headed exactly once.
let headerPending = destinationDetail;

// The previous destination section as RENDERED TEXT, so a consecutive repeat of
// the same movement prints its dates alone. Null until the first section.
let prevDestKey = null;
%>
<section id="items">
  <table>
    <% if (destinationDetail) { %>
    <colgroup><% for (let i = 0; i < columnCount; i++) { %><col><% } %></colgroup>
    <% } else { %>
    <thead>
      <%~ headerRow %>
    </thead>
    <% } %>
    <tbody>
      <%/* The banner spans this table exactly. It is `itemColumnCount` rather
           than a second copy of the arithmetic: the alignment edge of the page
           is derived from the same number, and a banner that disagreed with
           the header would take that edge with it. The base read `5` and every
           banner row spanned one column short of its own table. */%>
      <% const bannerSpan = columnCount; %>
      <% renderedItems.forEach(item => { %>
        <% const dest = (destinationDetail && item.type === "destination") ? destinations.find(p => p.uid === item.uid) : undefined; %>
        <% if (headerPending && !dest) { %>
        <%~ headerRow %>
        <% headerPending = false; } %>
        <% if (item.type === "group") { %>
        <tr>
          <td class="wide" colspan="<%= bannerSpan %>">
            <strong><%= item.name %></strong>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <% } else if (item.type === "order") { %>
        <% if (showOrderBanner) { %>
        <tr>
          <td class="wide" colspan="<%= bannerSpan %>">
            <strong><%= item.name %></strong>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <% } %>
        <% } else if (item.type === "destination") { %>
        <% if (dest) { %>
        <%/* The OrderDetail destination divider, as a PDF: ONE row holding a
             three-column grid, directly above the lines delivered there.

               Delivery:  place name      ->   collection place name (only if the sides differ)
                          address              collection address

             A destination REPEATING the one before it prints the date lines
             alone — both sides have to match for that.
                          contact - phone         collection contact - phone
                          instructions            collection instructions
               Active:    start -> end (clock) duration
               Charged:   start -> end (clock) days

             The LABEL column names the movement (`getDestinationsLegend`:
             Delivery or In Store Pickup) on the place line, and names the window
             on each date line. The date lines span both address columns, so a
             window reads start, arrow, end on one line.

             The window label is Dates: when there is one window and Active: when
             a Charged: window follows, so the two are only told apart when there
             are two. One Charged: line per charge window, and a Total charged
             line when there are several; the total is in BILLABLE days,
             Σ max(days, 5), so it agrees with the money.

             The contact and instructions lines are each omitted when both legs
             are empty. With no collection leg there is no right column and no
             arrow: a sale is handed over and never comes back.

             A SECOND COPY of the date rules in partials/shared/destinations.eta,
             deliberately: that table still serves the invoice, the pick sheet and
             the packing list. Keep the two in step until they are reconciled. */%>
        <%
          const d = dest.dates || {};
          const windows = d.charge_windows || [];
          const showCharged = hasCollection && windows.length > 0 &&
            !u.isSameAsDeliveryDates(d);
          // A 0-day window is valid, and `formatChargeDays` throws on 0.
          const chargePeriod = (n) => n > 0 ? it.dates.formatChargeDays(n).periodLabel : "0 days";
          const stamp = (iso) => it.dates.formatChicagoWeekdayDate(iso) + " " + it.dates.formatChicagoTime(iso);
          const addressText = (ep) => {
            if (!ep || !ep.address) return "";
            const a = ep.address;
            return [
              a.street, a.street2, a.city,
              [a.region, a.postcode].filter(Boolean).join(" "),
              (a.country_name && a.country_name !== "United States") ? a.country_name : null,
            ].filter(Boolean).join(", ");
          };
          // The place NAME and its ADDRESS are two lines. `placeText` joins them
          // only to compare the two sides; `placeLines` is what prints.
          const placeLines = (ep) => [(ep && ep.address && ep.address.name) || "", addressText(ep)].filter(Boolean);
          const placeText = (ep) => placeLines(ep).join("\n");
          const contactText = (ep) => {
            const c = ep && ep.contact;
            if (!c) return "";
            const phone = c.phones && c.phones.length > 0 ? c.phones[0] : null;
            return [c.name, phone].filter(Boolean).join(" - ");
          };
          const instructionsText = (ep) => (ep && ep.instructions) || "";
          // In Store, not the legend value In Store Pickup: this label names the
          // movement, and the Pickup half is the leg — which the places either
          // side of the arrow already say.
          const legLabel = (u.getDestinationsLegend([dest]).start || "Delivery")
            .replace("In Store Pickup", "In Store");
          // The collection side (arrow, place, contact, instructions) only when it
          // differs from the delivery side in ANY of the three, compared as
          // RENDERED text so the same address under a different key order still
          // reads as one place. When all three match it would repeat the left.
          const showReturn = hasCollection && (
            placeText(dest.collection) !== placeText(dest.delivery) ||
            contactText(dest.collection) !== contactText(dest.delivery) ||
            instructionsText(dest.collection) !== instructionsText(dest.delivery)
          );
          const returnContact = showReturn ? contactText(dest.collection) : "";
          const returnInstructions = showReturn ? instructionsText(dest.collection) : "";
          const hasContactLine = contactText(dest.delivery) !== "" || returnContact !== "";
          const hasInstructionsLine = instructionsText(dest.delivery) !== "" || returnInstructions !== "";
          const arrow = () => it.icons.svg("arrow-right", { size: 9 });
          const clock = () => it.icons.svg("clock", { size: 9 });
          // A DESTINATION THAT REPEATS THE ONE BEFORE IT SHOWS ITS DATES ONLY.
          // An order can split one venue across several legs that differ only by
          // when, and reprinting the address, the contact and the instructions
          // each time says nothing the reader did not just read. BOTH sides have
          // to match, so a leg coming back to a different place still prints in
          // full. Compared as rendered text, like showReturn above.
          const destKey = [
            placeText(dest.delivery), contactText(dest.delivery), instructionsText(dest.delivery),
            placeText(dest.collection), contactText(dest.collection), instructionsText(dest.collection),
          ].join("|");
          const sameAsPrev = destKey === prevDestKey;
          prevDestKey = destKey;
        %>
        <tr class="destination-section">
          <td class="wide" colspan="<%= columnCount %>">
            <div class="destination-grid<% if (!hasCollection) { %> one-leg<% } %>">
              <% if (!sameAsPrev) { %>
              <div class="label bold"><%= legLabel %>:</div>
              <div class="start"><% placeLines(dest.delivery).forEach((line, i) => { %><% if (i > 0) { %><br><% } %><%= line %><% }) %></div>
              <% if (showReturn) { %>
              <div class="arrow"><span class="inline-arrow"><%~ arrow() %></span></div>
              <div class="end"><% placeLines(dest.collection).forEach((line, i) => { %><% if (i > 0) { %><br><% } %><%= line %><% }) %></div>
              <% } %>
              <% if (hasContactLine) { %>
              <div class="label"></div>
              <div class="start"><%= contactText(dest.delivery) %></div>
              <% if (showReturn) { %><div class="end"><%= returnContact %></div><% } %>
              <% } %>
              <% if (hasInstructionsLine) { %>
              <div class="label"></div>
              <div class="start"><%= instructionsText(dest.delivery) %></div>
              <% if (showReturn) { %><div class="end"><%= returnInstructions %></div><% } %>
              <% } %>
              <% } %>
              <div class="label bold"><% if (showCharged) { %>Active:<% } else { %>Dates:<% } %></div>
              <div class="span">
                <% if (d.delivery_start) { %><%= stamp(d.delivery_start) %><% } %>
                <% if (hasCollection) { %>
                <% if (d.delivery_start && d.collection_start) { %><span class="inline-arrow"><%~ arrow() %></span><% } %>
                <% if (d.collection_start) { %><%= stamp(d.collection_start) %><% } %>
                <% if (d.days_active != null) { %><span class="inline-clock"><%~ clock() %></span><span class="destination-days"><%= it.dates.formatChargeDays(d.days_active).periodLabel %></span><% } %>
                <% } %>
              </div>
              <% if (showCharged) { %>
              <% windows.forEach((w, wi) => { %>
              <div class="label bold"><% if (wi === 0) { %>Charged:<% } %></div>
              <div class="span">
                <%= it.dates.formatChicagoWeekdayDate(w.start) %><span class="inline-arrow"><%~ arrow() %></span><%= it.dates.formatChicagoWeekdayDate(w.end) %>
                <span class="inline-clock"><%~ clock() %></span><span class="destination-days"><%= chargePeriod(w.days) %></span>
              </div>
              <% }) %>
              <% if (windows.length > 1) { %>
              <div class="label bold">Total charged:</div>
              <div class="span"><span class="inline-clock"><%~ clock() %></span><span class="destination-days"><%= chargePeriod(it.dates.billableDays(windows.map((w) => w.days))) %></span></div>
              <% } %>
              <% } %>
            </div>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <%/* The column headers belong to the section above them: the lines a
             destination introduces are headed under that destination, not under
             a header row printed once at the top of a table that spans several. */%>
        <%~ headerRow %>
        <% headerPending = false; %>
        <% } else if (showDestinationBanners) { %>
        <tr>
          <td class="wide" colspan="<%= bannerSpan %>">
            <strong><%= item.name %></strong>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <% } %>
        <% } else { %>
        <%/* The money on the row itself plus anything rolled onto it from a hidden
             zero-priced component. With the param off, `rolled` is undefined
             everywhere and these are the stored figures on the line. */%>
        <% const rolled = rolledUp.get(pathKey(item)); %>
        <% const rowTaxCents = itemTaxCents(item) + (rolled ? rolled.tax : 0); %>
        <% const rowSubtotalCents = (item.price.subtotal_discounted_cents ?? item.price.subtotal_cents) + (rolled ? rolled.subtotal : 0); %>
        <% const rowTotalCents = item.price.total_cents + (rolled ? rolled.total : 0); %>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding-left: <%= componentDepth(item) %>em"><%= item.name %></td>
          <td class="capitalize"><% if (DISAMBIGUATED_ITEM_TYPES.has(item.type)) { %><%= item.type %><% } %></td>
          <td><%= numberCell(item.quantity) %></td>
          <% if (hasRentals) { %>
          <td><%/* The unit is core's call, not this file's: `formatChargeDays`
                   auto-derives weeks at >= 5 chargeable days and days below it,
                   and singularizes, so a 10-day rental reads `2 weeks` here and
                   in the manager alike. It THROWS on <= 0, which the truthiness
                   guard in front of it already excludes.

                   A `fixed`-formula rental still carries a `chargeable_days`
                   count (core sets it from the booking regardless of formula),
                   but the price does not scale with it — so this checks
                   `formula` FIRST and never reaches the days formatter for one. */%><%= item.type !== "rental" ? '' : item.price.formula === "fixed" ? "Fixed" : item.price.chargeable_days ? it.dates.formatChargeDays(item.price.chargeable_days).periodLabel : '' %></td>
          <% } %>
          <td><%= moneyCellOn(item.price.base_cents, rowTaxCents) %></td>
          <% if (hasDiscount) { %>
          <td>
            <%/* Parenthesised: a discount is a deduction, and the accounting
                 convention reads it as one without a sign the eye can miss.
                 Both arms get the parens — a `percent` discount is the same
                 deduction expressed as a rate, so bracketing only the flat one
                 would make the column's two shapes look like different kinds
                 of number. Empty cell when there is no discount, NOT `(  )`.

                 Padded inside the brackets — `( 20% )`, not `(20%)`. At the 9px
                 root the glyphs sit tight enough that an unpadded bracket reads
                 as part of the number rather than around it. */%>
            <% if (item.price.discount) { %>
            <% if (item.price.discount.type === "percent") { %>
            <% if (item.price.discount.rate) { %>( <%= item.price.discount.rate %>% )<% } %>
            <% } else { %>
            <% if (item.price.discount.amount_cents) { %>( <%= it.money.formatCents(item.price.discount.amount_cents) %> )<% } %>
            <% } %>
            <% } %>
          </td>
          <% } %>
          <%
            // `??`, not `||`. A fully-discounted line has
            // `subtotal_discounted_cents: 0`, which is falsy — so `||` fell
            // through and printed the PRE-discount subtotal for exactly the
            // lines where the discount mattered most. The bug was invisible
            // because neither fixture carried a discount until templates#57.
          %>
          <td><%= moneyCellOn(rowSubtotalCents, rowTaxCents) %></td>
          <% if (hasTax) { %>
          <%/* The `taxes && taxes.length > 0` guard is gone because `moneyCell`
               subsumes it: an empty array reduces to 0 and renders blank, which
               is the same cell the guard produced. One test, not two. */%>
          <td><%= moneyCell(rowTaxCents) %></td>
          <% } %>
          <td class="total"><%= moneyCell(rowTotalCents) %></td>
        </tr>
        <% if (item.description) { %>
        <tr>
          <td class="wide" colspan="<%= bannerSpan %>" style="padding-left: <%= componentDepth(item) %>em; font-style: italic; font-weight: normal">
            <%= item.description %>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <% } %>
        <% } %>
      <% }) %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>
